**D176 — DEVDESK-711 (S-02b of `docs/product/safe-process-stop/`): on Linux and macOS, `K` on the Ports tab asks the process to stop (SIGTERM), watches it for up to 5 s without blocking, and sends SIGKILL only after a second confirmation. Decided (orchestrator, within the autonomy the user granted on 2026-11-23; the user can overrule): Q2, the grace is a fixed 5 s (`stopGrace`), not configurable; Q6, the label of `K` is `Stop process` on Linux and macOS and `Kill process` on Windows (the Windows label must not suggest a clean stop, `TestWindowsLabelsPromiseNoCleanStop`), the key stays `K`; a zombie counts as stopped; only one stop at a time; a row absent from the table can still be forced; the result of a SIGKILL is always announced, and `os.ErrProcessDone` on it reads as `<name> (<pid>) stopped`. **Exception to Rule 130, written here on purpose:** `K` is greyed on every row while a stop is in progress (`Another stop is already in progress`, or `This process is already being stopped` on its own row). Rule 130 says an operation in progress is not a cause of grey; criterion 7 of S-02b (one stop at a time, `K` unavailable with the reason) requires it. It lasts at most 5 s plus one modal, and pressing `K` puts the reason in the footer. `esc` follows the rule (always in the header on Linux and macOS, greyed outside the wait). Decided (design): (1) Where the wait lives (B): a state machine in `internal/ui/netdiag` (`pendingStop`, phases `signalling`, `waiting`, `askingForce`, `forcing`) over stateless primitives in `internal/ports` (`Terminate`, `Probe`, `Kill`); each probe is one `tea.Tick` of 250 ms and returns a message, so only `Update` changes the model (Rule 110). Rejected: a blocking `StopGracefully` in a `Cmd` (`esc` could not interrupt it and the second confirmation is UI state). (2) Sending (A): `realOS.kill(pid, want, sig)` is still the only place that signals, so SIGTERM and SIGKILL get the same held process and identity check, and `TestOnlyRealOSSendsASignal` is unchanged. Rejected: a second `realOS.term` (two dispatch points, two guards). (3) Probing (C): `Probe` reads the state and the identity stamp without any signal (`/proc/<pid>/stat` on Linux, `sysctl kern.proc.pid` on macOS), so it sees a zombie, which signal 0 does not, and a PID taken by another process reads as exited. Rejected: signal 0 (refused by the guard, blind to zombies), polling the pidfd (it would have to be held for 5 s between two `Cmd`s, and macOS has none). (4) Messages while off screen (C): `portsTermResultMsg`, `portsStopProbeMsg` and `portsKillResultMsg` implement `netdiag.BackgroundMsg`, and `internal/app` routes them to netdiag on screen or not (`routeNetdiagWork`). Rejected: dropping them (`K` greyed for good, row busy for good) and re-arming in `Init` (the SIGTERM result would be unknowable, the chain could double). (5) Every message carries the generation of its stop (package atomic counter): `esc`, a refused signal or a new stop makes the tick still in flight stale, and it is ignored. (6) The deadline is the instant the SIGTERM returned plus 5 s, compared with the tick instant: no clock in the model. (7) An unreadable probe is logged and counts as running: nothing is announced that was not seen. (8) The second `Yes` uses the identity captured at the first confirmation and recomputes the protection; a `replaced` row is refused with no system call, an `absent` one is not (a stopping process often closes its sockets before it exits); the SIGKILL verifies the identity again. (9) Windows is unchanged: `pm.stop` is never set, one confirmation, `Killing …`, `Process N terminated`; `Terminate` answers `ErrNoCleanStop`, `Probe` `errors.ErrUnsupported`, and the UI calls neither. Limits and risks: not run on macOS (`kern.proc.pid` on a zombie, `P_stat == SZOMB`, a constant copied from `<sys/proc.h>`, not verified; if the kernel does not return it the read is « gone » and the stop counts either way; SIP may refuse SIGTERM); the second confirmation can open while the user is on another tab or view and is not visible there; rebuilding the netdiag view during the wait loses it (`Running()` protects only a view opened from another view); the 5 s window of PID reuse is covered by the identity stamp, with the residual window of D175 at each signal; the second `Yes` relies on the identity check at the signal alone for an absent row. Known bug, not fixed here: the Windows confirmation of S-02a (a line of 89 cells) overflows an 80-column terminal, DEVDESK-735. Decided after the review and the security audit of S-02b (two findings): (10) A zombie on Linux is a state `Z` **and** `Threads: 1` in `/proc/<pid>/status` (`isLinuxZombie`, `readThreads`): the audit found that a process whose main thread called `pthread_exit` while other threads run reads `Z` on `/proc/<pid>/stat` and would have been announced as stopped while alive. A state `Z` with `Threads:` above 1, or one that cannot be read or parsed, reads as running (the safe side); a status file that is gone reads as exited; the file is read only when the state is `Z`; macOS is unchanged. Rejected: state `Z` alone (the finding). (11) The second confirmation drops its `y`/`Y` shortcut and opens on No (`ConfirmModal.WithoutYesShortcut()`, opt-in, every other modal unchanged), and `askForce` leaves the filter in edit mode first (`FilterBar.EndSearch()`, text kept) and puts the Warn `<name> (<pid>) did not exit within 5 s` in the footer: the audit found that the modal opens on a timer, with no user gesture, so a `y` typed in the filter would have sent a SIGKILL. Yes needs `←`/`→` then `enter`. Rejected: keeping `y` and relying on the focus on No (the letter answers Yes whatever the focus), and discarding the filter text (loses what the user typed). Limit added: the process name is shown unsanitised in four new places (the two confirmations, the row label, the footer), DEVDESK-720. No ADR: local, reversible by a revert, no schema, cache or configuration. **Plane:** DEVDESK-711; bug DEVDESK-735; DEVDESK-720. Number: D175 is the highest in this worktree; re-check at merge. Sources: `internal/ports/{ports,probe,procos,identity,identity_*}.go`, `internal/ui/netdiag/{ports_stop,ports_model,ports_commands,header,update}.go`, `internal/app/app.go`, `docs/architecture/network.md` (« Two-step stop »), `docs/architecture/app-shell.md`.**

