cket` (zero is ambiguous, the interface could interpret it). (4) The deferred read is synchronous in `Update`, at the press of `K`, Windows only; rejected: a `Cmd` and an intermediate message (more states: second `K`, moved selection, a late modal, for microseconds). (5) On a changed identity: a single re-read of the table (`portsRefreshMsg`, outside the tick chain, so no second chain starts), the footer says `<name> (PID N) is no longer the process that was listed — table refreshed`; no retry of the stop. (6) `PortsModel.refusal` is the one classifier of a refused stop, from the press of `K`, `Yes` and the kill result; `UnverifiableError` and `IdentityChangedError` are Warn (a precondition, Rule 128), `Refused by the system` stays Error. (7) macOS: `sysctl kern.proc.pid` answering `EIO`, `ESRCH`, `ENOENT` or a record with another `P_pid` is read as « process done » (the kernel answers with no data and `x/sys` says `EIO`; not run on macOS). Windows: `windowsBirthSlack` (100 ms) is a guess at the granularity of `CreationTime` and `time.Now`, not verified. Limits and risks: macOS and Linux without a pidfd keep a short window between the comparison and the signal (Go falls back to the bare PID silently); on Linux and macOS the identity is read after the socket snapshot, so a PID that changes hands in those milliseconds (a full turn of `pid_max`) would carry the newcomer's identity; a Windows clock set backwards between the listing and the read can make a younger process look older; `identity_windows.go` and `identity_darwin.go` are compiled for their OS and read, never run; `windowsIdentityBudget` is the story's starting value, not a measure; a Windows row over the budget is not greyed ahead of time when the system would refuse it, the refusal shows at `K`; `createTime` (ms) still feeds S-01a's parent rule and `Identity` is a second source, unifying them would change S-01a and is not done; `ports.Kill` now needs an identity, the zero value is refused (one production ca